# Holocron .gitignore
#
# Two conventions, both load-bearing:
#
#   * A trailing slash means directory-only, so a rule can never swallow a file.
#   * A LEADING slash anchors to the repo root, and is used for every rule whose
#     name could plausibly also be a source directory -- build, cache, logs,
#     presets, out, install, coverage. Without it, `build/` also ignores
#     src/build/ and cmake/build/, and the loss is silent.
#
# Anchoring bounds the blast radius but does NOT solve case-insensitivity. This
# machine is case-insensitive NTFS (core.ignorecase=true), so a rule `/Debug/`
# would also ignore a root-level `debug/` here while Linux CI tracks it happily --
# a divergence invisible on the host where the work happens. The fix is not
# cleverer patterns, it is not writing rules that collide with plausible source
# directory names in the first place. That is why there is no MSVC
# Debug/Release/x64 block below: CMake writes MSVC configuration output inside
# the build tree, which /build/ already covers, and those rules are inherited
# from hand-rolled .vcxproj layouts this project will never have.
#
# There is deliberately NO blanket *.toml, *.frag, *.glsl, *.png or *.jpg rule
# anywhere in this file. Those are first-party source.
#
# Sanity check before committing anything unusual:  git status --ignored


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CMake and build trees
#
# Anchored, because src/build/ and cmake/build/ are plausible source dirs.
# _deps/ and CMakeFiles/ are left unanchored on purpose: CMake genuinely writes
# them at arbitrary depth inside the build tree, and neither name is a credible
# first-party directory.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/build/
/build-*/
/cmake-build-*/
/out/
/install/

_deps/
CMakeFiles/
CMakeCache.txt
cmake_install.cmake
CTestTestfile.cmake
install_manifest.txt

# CMakePresets.json is committed and shared. CMakeUserPresets.json is per-dev.
CMakeUserPresets.json
CMakeSettings.json

# clangd / clang-tidy index and compile database. The .clang-format,
# .clang-tidy and .clangd config files themselves are NOT ignored.
compile_commands.json
.cache/
.ccache/


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compiler, linker, and profiling output
#
# *.map and *.user are deliberately absent: a linker map only ever appears in
# the build tree (already covered), while src/render/lut.map or a band-mapping
# table is entirely plausible first-party source.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

*.o
*.obj
*.gch
*.pch
*.a
*.lib
*.so
*.so.*
*.dylib
*.dll
*.exe
*.pdb
*.ilk
*.exp
*.dSYM/

*.gcda
*.gcno
*.profraw
*.profdata
/coverage/
perf.data
perf.data.old
callgrind.out.*

/core
core.[0-9]*
*.stackdump
vgcore.*


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Windows / MSVC  (the target -- see D-022, D-023)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

.vs/
*.vcxproj.user
*.suo
*.VC.db
*.VC.opendb
*.tlog
ipch/

# vcpkg manifest mode: vcpkg.json IS committed, the installed tree is not.
vcpkg_installed/
/vcpkg/


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OS cruft
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

.DS_Store
._*
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
.fseventsd

*~
.directory
.nfs*
.fuse_hidden*

Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editors and IDEs
#
# .vscode is NOT blanket-ignored -- shareable project config stays committed.
# The `.vscode/*` star form is required for the negations to work at all.
# c_cpp_properties.json stays ignored: it hardcodes local toolchain paths, and
# clangd + compile_commands.json is the shared setup.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

.vscode/*
!.vscode/settings.json
!.vscode/tasks.json
!.vscode/launch.json
!.vscode/extensions.json
*.code-workspace

.idea/
*.iml

*.xcodeproj/
*.xcworkspace/
xcuserdata/
DerivedData/

*.swp
*.swo
Session.vim
/tags

# Merge and patch debris. Near-certain to appear if libprojectM build patches
# are ever carried in-tree, and the classic accidental commit.
*.orig
*.rej
*.bak
*.tmp


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python (tooling scripts only -- no first-party Python yet)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECRETS AND LOCAL CONFIG  -- the highest-stakes block in this file
#
# gatekeeper.toml holds the audio device selection AND the Plex server address
# and auth token. An X-Plex-Token is a long-lived credential granting access to
# the whole library. Once it lands in a commit on a public repo that anyone has
# forked, rewriting history does not retract it: the fork keeps the object.
# The rule has to exist before the first commit, not after the first mistake.
#
# Unanchored on purpose -- a stray copy in a subdirectory is still a leaked
# token. A committed gatekeeper.example.toml template is unaffected by these
# patterns and is the intended way to document the schema.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

gatekeeper.toml
gatekeeper.local.toml
secrets.toml
*.token
plex_token*
.env
.env.*
!.env.example


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# libprojectM / MilkDrop preset packs  (M4)
#
# Tens of thousands of third-party files whose authors are mostly untraceable
# and who granted no license. Explicitly NOT vendored -- gatekeeper.toml points
# at wherever they live on disk. Ignored here so a stray `git add -A` during
# development cannot pull 40,000 files of someone else's work into a public repo.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

*.milk
*.prjm
/presets/
/milkdrop/
/projectm-presets/


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime caches, logs, and state  (M5 Plex, spdlog)
#
# Album art is fetched from Plex and cached on disk; it is third-party artwork
# and must never be committed or redistributed. These paths are a guess until
# M5 fixes them -- when the cache path is wired up in gatekeeper.toml, make sure
# whatever it points at in-tree is covered here.
#
# Images are NOT blanket-ignored: docs figures and crystal preview thumbnails
# are legitimate first-party source.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/cache/
/art-cache/
/albumart/
/logs/
*.log


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Media
#
# Broadly ignored so that dropping a FLAC in the tree to exercise `--file` mode
# cannot turn into a 40 MB blob in permanent history. Test fixtures are
# re-included wholesale below.
#
# Keep this list in sync with section 5 of .gitattributes, which marks the same
# formats binary. They describe the same set and will drift the first time
# someone adds a format to one and not the other.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Character-class form for the same reason as .gitattributes section 5: ignore
# patterns are case-SENSITIVE wherever core.ignorecase is false, which is every
# Linux checkout and all of CI. A plain `*.flac` does not match TRACK.FLAC there,
# and rippers do emit uppercase extensions -- so the plain form silently stops
# working in CI while continuing to look correct on the Windows target.

*.[wW][aA][vV]
*.[fF][lL][aA][cC]
*.[mM][pP]3
*.[mM]4[aA]
*.[aA][aA][cC]
*.[oO][gG][gG]
*.[oO][gG][aA]
*.[oO][pP][uU][sS]
*.[aA][iI][fF][fF]
*.[aA][iI][fF]
*.[wW][vV]
*.[aA][pP][eE]
*.[dD][sS][fF]
*.[dD][fF][fF]
*.[wW][mM][aA]
*.[mM][kK][aA]
*.[cC][aA][fF]
*.[mM][pP]4
*.[mM][kK][vV]
*.[mM][oO][vV]
*.[wW][eE][bB][mM]
*.[aA][vV][iI]

# Re-include the entire fixture tree. `**` rather than `*` on purpose: a
# single-level negation would rescue tests/fixtures/tone.wav but silently miss
# tests/fixtures/audio/tone.wav. tests/ itself is not ignored, so this works --
# note that a negation can never rescue a file whose PARENT DIRECTORY is
# ignored, which is a hard git rule with no workaround.
!tests/fixtures/**


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Source-extension guard
#
# Defensive negations for first-party source types. If someone later adds a
# blanket `*.frag` or `*.toml` rule ABOVE this block, these keep the vault and
# the engine shaders tracked.
#
# Honest limits: this only helps for a rule added above it, and it cannot
# rescue anything under an ignored directory. It is a cheap seatbelt, not a
# guarantee -- `git status --ignored` remains the real check.
#
# Engine shaders are covered as well as vault crystals: the compositor blit and
# blend passes, M1's debug facet, and M6's UI shaders are engine source and will
# not live in the vault.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

!**/*.frag
!**/*.vert
!**/*.geom
!**/*.comp
!**/*.glsl
!**/*.inc
!vault/**
!include/**
!src/**
!docs/**

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The compiled-program cache.
#
# GENERATED, MACHINE-SPECIFIC, AND NOT PORTABLE EVEN IN PRINCIPLE. A GL program
# binary is valid only for the driver and GPU that produced it -- that is why
# the cache keys on the vendor string -- so a committed one is dead weight on
# every machine except the one it came from.
#
# It was not ignored, and a run of the player left two .bin files staged in an
# unrelated commit. Found by reading `git status` rather than by anything
# failing; nothing here would have complained.
#
# `[render] shader_cache` can move this, so the directory name is matched
# anywhere rather than only at the root.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

shader-cache/
**/shader-cache/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Diagnostic captures from scripts/shield-capture.sh.
#
# The script writes a timestamped directory into the repo root, and what goes
# in it is whatever the device had: the run logs, a full logcat, the socket
# tables, the activity dump. That is a diagnosis, not a source file, and it
# belongs to the occurrence rather than to the project.
#
# IGNORED RATHER THAN RELOCATED because the script's own contract is that it
# runs from anywhere and leaves the app untouched -- and because a capture is
# something somebody reads once, under time pressure, while a fault is live.
# Asking them to route the output somewhere safe first is exactly the step that
# gets skipped.
#
# THIS IS ALSO A SECRETS GUARD. A logcat is a whole-device log; nothing
# guarantees another app has not printed something private into it, and this
# repository is public. An unignored capture directory is one `git add -A` away
# from being permanent.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

shield-capture-*/
